# Writing style

User-facing text is written the way Adam Hall writes: formal Australian English, specific, and direct. A sentence says what the thing is, who it is for, and what happens. It does not sell.

## Voice

- Australian spelling: organisation, specialised, artefact, recognised, favour. Practise is the verb and practice is the noun. Use program, not programme.
- Complete sentences for anything longer than a label. "No users yet. Add a person when they need access."
- Name the object. Prefer application in prose. A button can stay short: "Create an application".
- Operational detail belongs in the sentence: a path, a permission, a next step, a date.
- Second person or plain description. The product does not speak in the first person.

## Leave out

- Slogans and brochure lines: "the way your team actually does it", "seamless", "unlock", "empower", "delight", "robust", "cutting-edge", "leverage", "show up", "the shape of the work".
- Exclamation marks, emoji, winks, and ellipses used for tone.
- Template companies and template people: Acme, Contoso, Contosa, Fabrikam, Northwind, AdventureWorks, Widget, Osteria, Ada Lovelace, ada@example.com.

## Examples

Use a plain organisation, an ordinary surname, and a real place, the way a file note would.

- Organisation: Health. Slug: health. Hostname: health.example.com. Email: records@health.example.com.
- People: Reid, Nguyen, Walsh, Campbell.
- Places: Barton, Kingston, Civic, Braddon.

"For example, Health." "For example, health.example.com."

A next step is a note typed on a record: "Follow up the draft." "Call on Thursday." "File the copy."

## Shape

A help paragraph is one or two sentences. An empty state says what is missing, then what to do. A hint under a tile says what that screen is for.

Good: "This site keeps the applications, records, and rules for the work."

Good: "A record type sets the fields a record uses."

Good: "Enter the full hostname customers will use, such as health.example.com."
